      "text": "To find the value of the fourth term in the sequence where consecutive powers of 3 are added, we first need to understand the general form of the \\( n \\)-th term in the sequence. The sequence is given as follows:\n\n1. The first term is \\( 3^0 \\).\n2. The second term is \\( 3^0 + 3^1 \\).\n3. The third term is \\( 3^0 + 3^1 + 3^2 \\).\n4. The fourth term is \\( 3^0 + 3^1 + 3^2 + 3^3 \\).\n\nLet's denote the \\( n \\)-th term of the sequence by \\( a_n \\). From the problem, we see that:\n\n\\[ a_0 = 3^0 \\]\n\\[ a_1 = 3^0 + 3^1 \\]\n\\[ a_2 = 3^0 + 3^1 + 3^2 \\]\n\\[ a_3 = 3^0 + 3^1 + 3^2 + 3^3 \\]\n\nWe can observe that the \\( n \\)-th term of the sequence is a sum of the first \\( n \\) powers of 3. Therefore, the fourth term \\( a_3 \\) is the sum of the first four powers of 3:\n\n\\[ a_3 = 3^0 + 3^1 + 3^2 + 3^3 \\]\n\nTo find the value of \\( a_3 \\), we calculate each power of 3:\n\n\\[ 3^0 = 1 \\]\n\\[ 3^1 = 3 \\]\n\\[ 3^2 = 9 \\]\n\\[ 3^3 = 27 \\]\n\nAdding these values together, we get:\n\n\\[ a_3 = 1 + 3 + 9 + 27 \\]\n\nNow, let's perform the addition step-by-step:\n\n\\[ 1 + 3 = 4 \\]\n\\[ 4 + 9 = 13 \\]\n\\[ 13 + 27 = 40 \\]\n\nThus, the value of the fourth term of the sequence is:\n\n\\[ \\boxed{40} \\]",
